---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import AnswerBlock from '../../components/AnswerBlock.astro';
import { getProject } from '../../data/projects';

const project = getProject('geo-aeo-search-strategy');

const sections = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'definitions', label: 'Definitions' },
  { id: 'challenge', label: 'Challenge' },
  { id: 'role', label: 'My role' },
  { id: 'approach', label: 'The method' },
  { id: 'execution', label: 'What it looks like' },
  { id: 'measurement', label: 'Measurement' },
  { id: 'outcome', label: 'Outcome' },
  { id: 'why-it-matters', label: 'Why it matters' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  project={project}
  sections={sections}
>
  <section class="cs-section" id="overview">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      This is a practice area rather than a single engagement: the method I use to extend conventional
      search strategy into AI-driven discovery. It's the layer underneath my GEO/AEO work in
      multifamily and the conceptual basis for the AI visibility platform.
    </p>
    <p>
      The short version of the position: GEO and AEO are not a replacement for SEO. They're what
      happens when you take search fundamentals seriously enough that a machine summarizing your
      content gets it right.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="definitions">
    <h2>Definitions</h2>
    <p>
      These terms get used loosely. Here's what I mean by them, since the distinction affects what you
      actually do.
    </p>

    <AnswerBlock question="What is generative engine optimization (GEO)?" level={3}>
      <p>
        Generative engine optimization is the practice of making a brand accurately and consistently
        represented in answers generated by AI systems — Google AI Overviews, ChatGPT, Gemini,
        Perplexity and similar. It focuses on entity clarity, factual consistency across every source
        a system might retrieve, content structured for extraction, and measurement of whether the
        brand appears and gets cited.
      </p>
    </AnswerBlock>

    <AnswerBlock question="What is answer engine optimization (AEO)?" level={3}>
      <p>
        Answer engine optimization is the narrower practice of structuring content so it can be used
        directly as an answer to a specific question — self-contained explanations, clear question
        framing, factual precision and supporting structured data. AEO is largely about the shape of
        the content on the page; GEO additionally covers the entity and source ecosystem around it.
      </p>
    </AnswerBlock>

    <AnswerBlock question="How is this different from traditional SEO?" level={3}>
      <p>
        It shares most of its foundation. Crawlability, information architecture, intent-matched
        content and accurate structured data still determine most outcomes. What changes is the
        objective: instead of competing for a position in a list of links, you're competing to be the
        source a system trusts enough to assert and cite. That raises the penalty for inconsistent
        facts and for content whose meaning depends on surrounding context.
      </p>
    </AnswerBlock>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="challenge">
    <h2>Challenge</h2>
    <p>
      The practical problem with GEO right now is that the discourse runs ahead of the evidence. There
      is real pressure on brands to "do something about AI search," and a corresponding supply of
      tactics that are either repackaged SEO basics or speculation presented as technique.
    </p>
    <p>So the method has to hold two things at once:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Take the shift seriously.</strong> A meaningful and growing share of discovery now resolves in an answer rather than a click, and being absent from those answers is a real commercial risk.</li>
      <li><strong>Don't overclaim.</strong> Traditional search is not dead; organic search still drives substantial discovery. Anyone selling GEO on the premise that SEO is obsolete is selling something else.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The honest position is that GEO is mostly strong search fundamentals, executed with more
      precision, plus a genuinely new measurement problem.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="role">
    <h2>My role</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Methodology</strong> — defining the sequence of work and what evidence justifies each step</li>
      <li><strong>Entity strategy</strong> — establishing canonical facts and reconciling them across sources</li>
      <li><strong>Content structure</strong> — designing page and answer patterns that are extractable without losing nuance</li>
      <li><strong>Structured data</strong> — schema implementation that describes the entity rather than decorating the page</li>
      <li><strong>Measurement design</strong> — turning "are we visible in AI search?" into something checkable over time</li>
      <li><strong>Internal education</strong> — explaining the distinction between GEO, AEO and SEO to stakeholders deciding where to spend</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="approach">
    <h2>The method</h2>

    <h3>1. Establish the canonical entity</h3>
    <p>
      Decide, once, what the authoritative version of every important fact is: legal and trade name,
      address, contact details, what the organization offers, who it serves, the policies and
      attributes people ask about. This sounds trivial and is almost never already true.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Audit every source a system might retrieve</h3>
    <p>
      The owned website is one input among many. Listings, directories, review platforms, social
      profiles, press mentions and partner sites all contribute. The audit catalogs where the brand
      appears and flags every place a fact disagrees with the canonical version.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Reconcile, starting with the highest-trust sources</h3>
    <p>
      Fix the conflicts, prioritizing the sources that answer engines lean on most heavily — the
      owned site and the primary local and industry records. Consistency across sources is the single
      highest-leverage GEO action available, and it's unglamorous enough that most competitors skip
      it.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Inventory the real question set</h3>
    <p>
      Collect the questions people actually ask, in their words: from Search Console queries, sales
      and leasing conversations, reviews, on-site search, and the "people also ask" surfaces. Group
      by intent rather than keyword.
    </p>

    <h3>5. Build answer-retrievable content</h3>
    <p>
      Each important question gets a clear, self-contained answer: the question framed the way people
      ask it, a direct response in the first sentence or two, and the supporting detail after.
      Self-contained matters — an answer that only makes sense in the context of the paragraph above
      it is hard to extract correctly.
    </p>

    <h3>6. Describe the entity in structured data</h3>
    <p>
      Schema that states what the thing is and how it relates to other entities, matching the visible
      content exactly. Structured data isn't a ranking trick; it's the least ambiguous way to tell a
      machine what a page is about.
    </p>

    <h3>7. Monitor presence and citation</h3>
    <p>
      Run the question set against answer engines on a regular cadence and record what gets said,
      whether the brand is named, whether the facts asserted are correct, and which sources get
      cited. This is the part that doesn't exist in conventional SEO workflows.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="execution">
    <h2>What it looks like in practice</h2>
    <p>
      Applied to a multifamily property, the method produces concrete work: a reconciled set of
      property facts across the website, Google Business Profile and listing platforms; FAQ content
      built from the questions renters actually ask about pets, parking, applications and lease terms;
      property and local business schema that matches the page; neighborhood content written in the
      vocabulary of that submarket; and a recurring check on what assistants say when asked about
      apartments in that market.
    </p>
    <p>
      Applied to an organization like a housing nonprofit, the same method produces a clear page per
      program, unambiguous service descriptions, consistent organizational facts, and resource content
      structured so a specific question gets a specific answer.
    </p>
    <p>
      In both cases almost none of the work is exotic. The discipline is in refusing to leave facts
      inconsistent and refusing to write content whose meaning depends on context a machine won't
      carry over.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="measurement">
    <h2>Measurement</h2>
    <p>
      The honest caveat: AI answer measurement is less stable than rank tracking. Answers vary by
      phrasing, personalization and model updates, so individual observations are noisy. That argues
      for measuring patterns over time rather than treating a single answer as a result.
    </p>
    <p>What I track:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Presence rate</strong> — how often the brand is named across the question set</li>
      <li><strong>Accuracy</strong> — whether asserted facts match the canonical record</li>
      <li><strong>Citation set</strong> — which sources answers draw from, and whether owned properties are in it</li>
      <li><strong>Coverage</strong> — the share of priority questions that have answerable content</li>
      <li><strong>Competitive presence</strong> — who is named instead, for which question clusters</li>
      <li><strong>Conventional baseline</strong> — Search Console and GA4, because organic search still matters and these shifts should be read together</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="outcome">
    <h2>Outcome</h2>
    <p>What this method delivers:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A defensible definition of GEO and AEO that stakeholders can make budget decisions against</li>
      <li>A canonical fact record and a reconciled source footprint</li>
      <li>Content patterns that work for human readers and answer extraction simultaneously</li>
      <li>Structured data that describes entities rather than decorating pages</li>
      <li>A repeatable monitoring cadence for AI presence, accuracy and citation</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Measured impact of the method: <span class="todo">[ADD VERIFIED RESULT]</span> — presence,
      citation or visibility figures to be added where they can be verified and shared.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="why-it-matters">
    <h2>Why it matters</h2>
    <p>
      Search is expanding. Google still matters; AI Overviews, ChatGPT, Gemini and Perplexity now
      matter alongside it. The organizations that handle this well won't be the ones that found a GEO
      trick — they'll be the ones whose facts are consistent, whose content answers real questions
      directly, and who can tell whether any of it is working.
    </p>
    <p>
      That's the version of this discipline I want to practice: unglamorous, verifiable, and honest
      about what's known versus assumed.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
